1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,829
There's one column missing from almost every grant tracking spreadsheet I've seen.

2
00:00:04,829 --> 00:00:07,655
It's also the first thing an auditor asks about.

3
00:00:07,655 --> 00:00:11,130
Stick around and I'll hand you the finished template, free.

4
00:00:11,130 --> 00:00:14,781
But first, the column that quietly gets nonprofits in trouble.

5
00:00:14,781 --> 00:00:19,197
If you're running your grants on a spreadsheet, this is not a knock on you.

6
00:00:19,197 --> 00:00:21,670
A spreadsheet is the right place to start.

7
00:00:21,670 --> 00:00:25,675
The trouble is that most grant trackers get built like a to-do list.

8
00:00:25,675 --> 00:00:28,030
Applied, approved, money received, done.

9
00:00:28,030 --> 00:00:34,508
But your funder and your auditor are asking a completely different question, and a to-do list can't answer it.

10
00:00:34,508 --> 00:00:39,160
So over the next ten minutes, let's build a grant tracking spreadsheet together

11
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:40,220
from a blank sheet

12
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:44,695
the way it should be built if you ever expect to sit across from an auditor.

13
00:00:44,695 --> 00:00:47,050
The finished template is genuinely free.

14
00:00:47,050 --> 00:00:53,469
Drop your email at the link in the description and I'll send you both the Google Sheets and the Excel version

15
00:00:53,469 --> 00:00:56,413
with every column we build today already in place.

16
00:00:56,413 --> 00:00:58,533
Quick note on where I'm coming from.

17
00:00:58,533 --> 00:01:00,477
I build grant compliance software

18
00:01:00,477 --> 00:01:04,069
so I'm going to show you the structure that survives an audit

19
00:01:04,069 --> 00:01:07,190
not just the one that looks tidy on a Monday morning.

20
00:01:07,190 --> 00:01:11,017
Everything I show you here works in plain Google Sheets or Excel.

21
00:01:11,017 --> 00:01:12,725
No add-ons, no special tools.

22
00:01:12,725 --> 00:01:15,250
Picture a typical grant tracker for a second.

23
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:19,066
Grant name, funder, award amount, a status column, maybe a due date.

24
00:01:19,066 --> 00:01:24,397
All of it tracks whether the money showed up, and nothing about how you're allowed to spend it.

25
00:01:24,397 --> 00:01:26,586
That sheet falls apart in three places.

26
00:01:26,586 --> 00:01:30,177
You can't tell which dollars are promised to a specific program.

27
00:01:30,177 --> 00:01:35,452
You know the award was fifty thousand, but not that you've already burned through forty-eight.

28
00:01:35,452 --> 00:01:39,043
And when a number changes, nobody knows who changed it, or when.

29
00:01:39,043 --> 00:01:41,681
By the end of this, we'll have fixed all three.

30
00:01:41,681 --> 00:01:42,354
Let's build.

31
00:01:42,354 --> 00:01:43,428
Open a new sheet.

32
00:01:43,428 --> 00:01:45,765
Your first tab is the Grant Register.

33
00:01:45,765 --> 00:01:48,798
One row per grant, and this is your master list.

34
00:01:48,798 --> 00:01:51,578
These are the columns that earn their place.

35
00:01:51,578 --> 00:01:56,632
Start with a Grant ID, a short code like E-D dash twenty-twenty-six dash oh-one.

36
00:01:56,632 --> 00:01:59,854
The funder prefix, the year, then a running number.

37
00:01:59,854 --> 00:02:05,982
You'll thank yourself the day you have twenty grants and three of them come from the same funder.

38
00:02:05,982 --> 00:02:12,173
Then grant name, funder, and funder type, whether that's federal, state, foundation, or corporate.

39
00:02:12,173 --> 00:02:16,279
The funder type matters because the rules change with the source.

40
00:02:16,279 --> 00:02:21,839
A federal award comes with a stack of requirements a local family foundation never will.

41
00:02:21,839 --> 00:02:25,187
Then the award amount, a start date, and an end date.

42
00:02:25,187 --> 00:02:26,640
That window has a name.

43
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:28,978
It's called the period of performance

44
00:02:28,978 --> 00:02:34,032
and spending outside that window is one of the more common things auditors flag.

45
00:02:34,032 --> 00:02:38,960
Money you spend the week before the grant officially starts can get disallowed

46
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:42,371
even when the expense itself was perfectly reasonable.

47
00:02:42,371 --> 00:02:46,920
Last, a real status column: Applied, Awarded, Active, Reporting, Closed.

48
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:49,699
A grant isn't finished when the money lands.

49
00:02:49,699 --> 00:02:52,732
It's finished when the final report is accepted.

50
00:02:52,732 --> 00:02:54,501
The register's taking shape.

51
00:02:54,501 --> 00:03:01,008
But we still haven't added the column from the start of this video, and it's the one that matters most.

52
00:03:01,008 --> 00:03:01,766
That's next.

53
00:03:01,766 --> 00:03:04,988
Now the column from the very start of this video.

54
00:03:04,988 --> 00:03:08,933
Add one called Restricted, with a simple yes-or-no dropdown.

55
00:03:08,933 --> 00:03:11,695
Right next to it, add Restriction Purpose.

56
00:03:11,695 --> 00:03:13,931
This one column is the whole game.

57
00:03:13,931 --> 00:03:20,638
A restricted grant is money a funder gave you for one specific purpose, say your after-school program.

58
00:03:20,638 --> 00:03:25,241
You're obligated, legally or by contract, to spend it on that purpose.

59
00:03:25,241 --> 00:03:28,989
Unrestricted money you can put toward general operations.

60
00:03:28,989 --> 00:03:34,841
Now, a restricted grant can usually cover a fair share of your overhead, things like rent

61
00:03:34,841 --> 00:03:38,129
but only when the award budget actually allows it.

62
00:03:38,129 --> 00:03:42,666
Charge general costs the grant never approved, and that isn't a typo.

63
00:03:42,666 --> 00:03:44,639
That's a compliance violation.

64
00:03:44,639 --> 00:03:49,899
If your organization spends a million dollars or more in federal money in a year

65
00:03:49,899 --> 00:03:52,332
the place that catches it has a name.

66
00:03:52,332 --> 00:03:57,198
It's called the single audit, and we'll do a whole video on that one soon.

67
00:03:57,198 --> 00:04:02,130
A spreadsheet without this column can't tell you whether you're in trouble.

68
00:04:02,130 --> 00:04:05,089
So we add it now, to every grant, on day one.

69
00:04:05,089 --> 00:04:11,730
The Restriction Purpose field next to it is where you write the actual sentence from the award letter

70
00:04:11,730 --> 00:04:12,914
in plain language.

71
00:04:12,914 --> 00:04:17,254
Future you, eighteen months from now, will not remember the terms.

72
00:04:17,254 --> 00:04:18,174
The cell will.

73
00:04:18,174 --> 00:04:19,095
One small tip.

74
00:04:19,095 --> 00:04:23,501
Use Data, then Data validation, to make Restricted a real dropdown.

75
00:04:23,501 --> 00:04:26,262
It stops the typos before they ever happen

76
00:04:26,262 --> 00:04:31,720
and a typo here is the kind of thing that quietly breaks a formula three tabs away.

77
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:34,757
Next tab, and this is the one that protects you.

78
00:04:34,757 --> 00:04:36,085
Budget versus Actual.

79
00:04:36,085 --> 00:04:42,222
Here you track spending against a single grant, not your whole organization, just this one award.

80
00:04:42,222 --> 00:04:46,461
Down the left side, list the budget categories the funder approved.

81
00:04:46,461 --> 00:04:50,193
Personnel, supplies, travel, whatever the award spells out.

82
00:04:50,193 --> 00:04:52,155
Match their wording, not yours.

83
00:04:52,155 --> 00:04:55,444
If the award says "Personnel, " don't call it "Staff

84
00:04:55,444 --> 00:05:00,569
" because at report time you want your line items to line up with theirs exactly.

85
00:05:00,569 --> 00:05:04,744
Then three columns across the top: Budgeted, Spent, and Remaining.

86
00:05:04,744 --> 00:05:09,110
Remaining is just budgeted minus spent, so let the sheet do the math.

87
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:11,450
Equals B2 minus C2, and copy it down.

88
00:05:11,450 --> 00:05:14,930
Why break it out by category instead of one big number?

89
00:05:14,930 --> 00:05:19,801
Because many funders approve your budget by category, not just as a lump sum.

90
00:05:19,801 --> 00:05:23,914
Say they gave you eight thousand for travel and you spend twelve.

91
00:05:23,914 --> 00:05:28,848
Your grand total might still be under budget, but you've overspent a category.

92
00:05:28,848 --> 00:05:30,873
With federal awards, for example

93
00:05:30,873 --> 00:05:35,554
once you shift more than about ten percent of the total between categories

94
00:05:35,554 --> 00:05:38,401
you usually need the funder's sign-off first.

95
00:05:38,401 --> 00:05:44,538
Tracking by line is how you catch that in March, instead of finding out at the audit in November.

96
00:05:44,538 --> 00:05:47,531
So how does that Spent number fill itself in?

97
00:05:47,531 --> 00:05:48,862
With an expense log.

98
00:05:48,862 --> 00:05:52,587
One tab, one row for every dollar you charge to a grant.

99
00:05:52,587 --> 00:05:54,117
The columns are simple.

100
00:05:54,117 --> 00:05:58,907
Date, Grant ID, Category, Vendor, Amount, and a Notes field for the why.

101
00:05:58,907 --> 00:06:02,499
The Grant ID is the key that ties everything together.

102
00:06:02,499 --> 00:06:07,222
It's how each expense connects back to the right grant in your register

103
00:06:07,222 --> 00:06:11,213
and it's why we gave every grant a code back in chapter two.

104
00:06:11,213 --> 00:06:17,266
Over on the Budget versus Actual tab, your Spent column becomes one formula, called SUMIFS.

105
00:06:17,266 --> 00:06:19,196
Think of it as a smart total.

106
00:06:19,196 --> 00:06:25,781
It scans your whole expense log and pulls only the rows that match this grant and this budget line.

107
00:06:25,781 --> 00:06:28,775
Log a receipt, and the budget updates itself.

108
00:06:28,775 --> 00:06:32,367
That single connection, expense log feeding the budget

109
00:06:32,367 --> 00:06:39,418
is the difference between a spreadsheet you actually trust and one you rebuild from scratch every quarter.

110
00:06:39,418 --> 00:06:43,476
Get this part right and you stop doing math by hand entirely.

111
00:06:43,476 --> 00:06:48,997
You just log expenses as they happen, and every other tab stays current on its own.

112
00:06:48,997 --> 00:06:52,241
Funders don't only want their money spent correctly.

113
00:06:52,241 --> 00:06:54,861
They want to hear about it, on a schedule.

114
00:06:54,861 --> 00:06:56,296
So add a Reporting tab.

115
00:06:56,296 --> 00:07:00,663
Grant ID, report type, due date, the date you submitted, and a status.

116
00:07:00,663 --> 00:07:04,219
Now for a genuinely useful trick: conditional formatting.

117
00:07:04,219 --> 00:07:06,902
Select your due-date column and add a rule.

118
00:07:06,902 --> 00:07:10,583
If a date falls within the next thirty days, turn it amber.

119
00:07:10,583 --> 00:07:14,638
If it's already passed and nothing's been submitted, turn it red.

120
00:07:14,638 --> 00:07:19,941
Your deadlines start finding you, instead of you trying to remember them at midnight.

121
00:07:19,941 --> 00:07:25,681
A missed report is one of the fastest ways to lose a renewal, and it's completely avoidable.

122
00:07:25,681 --> 00:07:28,987
And put a tiny dashboard at the top of your register.

123
00:07:28,987 --> 00:07:34,602
Four numbers: total awarded, total spent, the percentage of your funding that's restricted

124
00:07:34,602 --> 00:07:37,908
and how many reports are due in the next thirty days.

125
00:07:37,908 --> 00:07:40,841
Those are just a few SUM and COUNTIFS formulas.

126
00:07:40,841 --> 00:07:44,147
That's your entire grant portfolio in a single glance

127
00:07:44,147 --> 00:07:48,951
which is exactly the view your executive director or your board will ask for.

128
00:07:48,951 --> 00:07:53,897
Before we talk about where this breaks, here are two minutes that will save you.

129
00:07:53,897 --> 00:07:57,729
First, protect the cells that should never change by accident.

130
00:07:57,729 --> 00:08:01,500
In Google Sheets that's Data, then Protect sheets and ranges.

131
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:04,035
Lock your award amounts and your formulas

132
00:08:04,035 --> 00:08:09,475
so a well-meaning volunteer can't overwrite the math while they're fixing a vendor name.

133
00:08:09,475 --> 00:08:12,319
Second, give people the right level of access.

134
00:08:12,319 --> 00:08:16,584
Anyone who only needs to read the numbers gets View access, not Edit.

135
00:08:16,584 --> 00:08:21,282
Fewer hands on the keys means fewer mystery changes you can't explain later.

136
00:08:21,282 --> 00:08:26,599
Third, name the file like someone will ask you about it, because someday someone will.

137
00:08:26,599 --> 00:08:30,246
Pick one pattern, something like Grant Tracker and the year

138
00:08:30,246 --> 00:08:33,337
and save a dated copy at the end of every quarter.

139
00:08:33,337 --> 00:08:38,839
When a funder asks what your budget looked like back in June, you can actually show them.

140
00:08:38,839 --> 00:08:40,446
None of this is glamorous.

141
00:08:40,446 --> 00:08:46,072
It's the difference between a spreadsheet you can defend and one you have to apologize for.

142
00:08:46,072 --> 00:08:52,783
I make grant software for a living, so let me be straight with you about where this spreadsheet stops working.

143
00:08:52,783 --> 00:08:54,674
Because at some point, it will.

144
00:08:54,674 --> 00:08:58,639
The first crack shows up when more than one person edits the file

145
00:08:58,639 --> 00:09:02,727
and you genuinely can't tell who changed the award amount, or when.

146
00:09:02,727 --> 00:09:07,607
There's no audit trail, and the trail is the first thing an auditor asks to see.

147
00:09:07,607 --> 00:09:11,816
The second comes when a single expense has to split across two grants

148
00:09:11,816 --> 00:09:14,562
and no clean formula can allocate it cleanly.

149
00:09:14,562 --> 00:09:16,087
The third is just volume.

150
00:09:16,087 --> 00:09:20,845
Enough grants, and that reporting tab quietly becomes someone's full-time job.

151
00:09:20,845 --> 00:09:22,980
None of that is you doing it wrong.

152
00:09:22,980 --> 00:09:29,447
It's a spreadsheet doing exactly what a spreadsheet does, right up until your compliance load outgrows it.

153
00:09:29,447 --> 00:09:34,384
When you hit that wall, the next step is software that keeps the audit trail for you.

154
00:09:34,384 --> 00:09:37,869
Every change logged on its own, with a name and a timestamp.

155
00:09:37,869 --> 00:09:43,677
Restricted funds kept separate by the system, so you physically can't spend them on the wrong thing.

156
00:09:43,677 --> 00:09:46,639
Deadlines tracked without anyone babysitting a tab.

157
00:09:46,639 --> 00:09:48,788
That's exactly why I built GrantPipe.

158
00:09:48,788 --> 00:09:52,040
It's the same structure you just put in this spreadsheet

159
00:09:52,040 --> 00:09:55,874
except the audit trail and the fund accounting are handled for you

160
00:09:55,874 --> 00:09:59,010
instead of resting on whoever remembers to be careful.

161
